To make a cross-origin resource request (CORS) from your JavaScript code using the `fetch()` API, you need to ensure that the server you're trying to access has the appropriate CORS headers set up. If the server doesn't set these headers correctly, the browser will block your code from accessing the response. The browser's same-origin policy enforces CORS.

Here's the basic syntax of the `fetch()` method:
```javascript
fetch(url)
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));
```
To bypass the restrictions when running a local development server, you can use two common techniques:

1. Proxy Server in your backend:

If you have a backend server that does CORS, you can proxy your requests to the target server. This means you would call your own server, which will call the target server. Your server will receive the response and forward it back to your frontend.

Here is an example using Express.js in a Node.js environment:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/api', (req, res) => {
  const target = req.query.target;
  const url = `https://${target}/api/data`;

  fetch(url)
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => res.send(data))
    .catch(error => {
      console.error('Error:', error);
      res.status(500).send('Server Error');
    });
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Proxy server is running on port 3000'));
```
Now, you can call the target API using the following URL: `http://localhost:3000/api?target=api.example.com`

2. Enable CORS in your server:

If the server you're trying to access provides a way for you to edit its settings, you can add the necessary headers to enable CORS. However, this is not always possible, as you may not have direct access to the server's configuration.

Here's an example of how to enable CORS in a Node.js/Express.js server:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use(function(req, res, next) {
  res.header("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", "*");
  res.header("Access-Control-Allow-Headers", "Origin, X-Requested-With, Content-Type, Accept");
  res.header("Access-Control-Allow-Methods", "GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS");
  res.header("Allow", "GET,HEAD,POST,PUT,DELETE,OPTIONS");
  next();
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server is running on port 3000'));
```
In this example, we use the `app.use()` method to set the CORS headers for incoming requests.

Remember, by allowing all origins and methods, you are exposing your server to potential security risks. In a production environment, it's essential to limit the allowed origins and methods to trusted sources and necessary operations.

