```javascript
const runOnce = (function() {
  let executed = false;

  return function() {
    if (!executed) {
      executed = true;
      // Your code here that should run once
      console.log('This function will only run once');
    }
  };
})();

runOnce(); // Output: "This function will only run once"
runOnce(); // No output, as the function has already been executed
```

In this example, the `runOnce` variable stores the result of an IIFE, which returns an inner function. The `executed` variable, defined within the IIFE, is kept private using closure, ensuring it won't be affected by other code.

The inner function checks if `executed` is set to `false`, and if yes, sets it to `true` and runs the desired code. This ensures that subsequent calls to `runOnce()` will not execute the code again.

