#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  int i;

  for (i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {
    if (i % 3 == 0 && i % 5 == 0) {
      printf("FizzBuzz\n");
    } else if (i % 3 == 0) {
      printf("Fizz\n");
    } else if (i % 5 == 0) {
      printf("Buzz\n");
    } else {
      printf("%d\n", i);
    }
  }

  return 0;
}
```

Implementing a visually appealing and user-friendly web page layout that includes a cutting-edge voice recognition system involves integrating both frontend and backend technologies. You can achieve this using the SpeechRecognition gem in Ruby and other frontend libraries. However, it's important to note that the SpeechRecognition gem was last updated in 2014 and may not be suitable for all modern applications.

Here's a step-by-step guide on how to create a visually appealing web page layout with a voice recognition system using Ruby on Rails, the Web Speech API for voice recognition, and modern web technologies for user experience:

1. Set up a new Ruby on Rails project:

```
$ rails new voice_recognition_app
$ cd voice_recognition_app
```

2. Add necessary libraries to your Gemfile:

For a visually appealing layout, you can use Bootstrap. Add this line to your `Gemfile`:
``` ruby
gem 'bootstrap', '~> 5.1'
```

Run `bundle install` to install the Bootstrap gem.

3. Scaffold a simple webpage to demonstrate voice recognition:

Run `rails generate scaffold Article title:string content:text` to generate a simple scaffold for managing articles.

Set the root route in the `config/routes.rb` file:
```ruby
Rails.application.routes.draw do
  resources :articles
  root 'articles#index'
end
```

4. Apply Bootstrap styling:

Modify `app/assets/stylesheets/application.scss` and `app/assets/javascripts/application.js` to include and initialize Bootstrap.

application.scss:
```scss
@import "bootstrap/scss/bootstrap";
```

application.js:
```js
import 'bootstrap';
```

5. Implement the Web Speech API for voice recognition:

Since the SpeechRecognition gem is outdated, it is recommended that you utilize the Web Speech API that comes with modern web browsers. Add the following script in the `app/views/articles/index.html.erb` to enable voice recognition:

```html
<script>
  // Initialize SpeechRecognition
  const SpeechRecognition = window.SpeechRecognition || window.webkitSpeechRecognition;
  const recognition = new SpeechRecognition();

  recognition.continuous = false;
  recognition.lang = 'en-US';

  // Prepare voice command elements
  const voiceBtn = document.getElementById('voice-btn');
  const result = document.getElementById('voice-result');

  // Event listeners
  voiceBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    recognition.start();
  });

  recognition.addEventListener('result', (e) => {
    const transcript = e.results[0][0].transcript;
    result.textContent = transcript;
  });

  recognition.addEventListener('end', () => {
    // Process the voice command here
  });
</script>
```

Add a button and a placeholder to show the voice recognition result on the same page:

```html
<button id="voice-btn">Speak</button>
<div id="voice-result"></div>
```

6. Add a Polyfill for compatibility with older browsers:

Since the Web Speech API isn't supported in all browsers, you can use polyfills such as the `speech-polyfill` npm package to ensure compatibility. Install the package:

```sh
$ yarn add speech-polyfill
```

Include the polyfill in `app/assets/javascripts/application.js`:

```js
import 'speech-polyfill';
```

7. Handling variations in accents, dialects, and speech patterns:

You can manage variations by setting the `recognition.lang` property in the Web Speech API to the desired language or by implementing the `'voicestart'` event if further control is needed.

Example (switch languages):

```js
recognition.lang = 'en-GB'; // For British English
```

Now, the Web Speech API in your Rails application should be able to recognize voice commands and display them on the screen. It can also handle accents, dialects, and speech patterns based on the language and dialect settings provided. Keep in mind that this is only the beginning, as you may need to further refine and customize your application to handle more specific use cases and user interactions.

