In this tutorial, I'll walk you through an example of using Puppeteer to get web page content.

1. Install Puppeteer:
To start, you'll need to have Node.js (>=10.18.1) installed on your system. Next, create a new folder for your project and initialize it using the `npm init -y` command. After that, install Puppeteer using the following command:

```
npm install puppeteer
```

2. Create a JavaScript file:
Create a new file called `scrape.js` (or any name you prefer) in your project folder.

3. Import Puppeteer and write the scraping code:
Open the `scrape.js` file in your text editor and paste the following code:

```javascript
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

(async () => {
  // Launch a new browser instance
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage();

  // Navigate to the target web page
  await page.goto('https://example.com');

  // Get the web page content as HTML
  const content = await page.content();
  console.log(content);

  // Close the browser instance
  await browser.close();
})();
```

4. Analyze the code:
This script does the following:
- Imports the `puppeteer` module.
- Launches a new headless browser instance.
- Opens a new page (tab) in the browser.
- Navigates to "https://example.com".
- Retrieves the whole web page content as an HTML string and logs it to the console.
- Closes the browser instance.

5. Run the script:
Save the file and execute the script using Node.js:

```
node scrape.js
```

If everything is set up correctly, you'll see the web page source code for "https://example.com" in your console.

Now you've successfully used Puppeteer to fetch web page content. You can edit the code to scrape other websites or extract specific data elements based on your needs. Remember to always consider website terms and policies before scraping, and be respectful of server load to avoid causing unintended disruption.

